# Strict-parity flags

These five independent switches select the recorded Ruby paths listed below.
There is no universal legacy-compatibility switch. They default OFF and do not
establish whole-port compatibility, arbitrary mod support, migration, host
configuration, media availability or equivalence outside their measured cases.
The side-effects battery (`tools/parity-side-effects/scenarios.json`, run by
`tests/tools/side-effects-scenario-battery.test.ts`) exercises the first four
against its committed recording. The fifth has separate follow-rule tests and
browser-journey evidence; it is absent from that battery.

That named battery is distinct from the reference runner's
`reference-harness/fixtures/synthetic-v1/ops/scenarios.json`. Synthetic-v1
side-effect comparisons include notification mail, so the fifth switch's
mail-link branch is relevant there: OFF appends `success[redirect]=soft`,
while ON selects the bare reference link. A mail-byte comparison does not
also prove the dashboard refusal or follow-rule permission branch. Do not
describe the fifth switch as absent from every side-effect comparison.

The retained synthetic-v1 diagnostic comparison for #111 identifies these
seven notification-mail steps: `create-topic-with-fields-as-admin#1`,
`update-content-adds-link#1`, `rename-referenced-tag#1`, `delete-note#1`,
`delete-topic-with-fields#1`, `create-compound-on-new-left#1` and
`update-then-render#1`. Its raw strict-mode mail objects equal the recorded
expected objects; each raw default-mode object differs only by the encoded
soft-redirect suffix. Both comparisons explicitly declare dirty-source
diagnostic status, source qualification false and grader exit 2. These facts
explain the selected mail behavior; they do not close #111 or qualify a release.
The checker must independently admit the exact candidate source, fixture,
reference and comparison configuration before those answers become candidate
evidence. Enabling flags does not supply that admission or authorize changing
expected answers, normalization or exemptions.

The reference paths here target card **1.110.0** / Decko **0.20.0**, with the
particular fixture, configuration and capture named by each instrument.
Changing those pins or selecting all five switches requires a new comparison;
it does not qualify a different Ruby version, deck or mod set.

## The surface

One setting per flag, read from the host's settings bag: the `DECKO_*`
environment variables on a Node host, the same-named bindings/`[vars]` on the
Cloudflare and Bunny edges (`WriteSurfaceFlags`,
`deploy/bunny-worker-src/bunny-edge-signin-session-surface.ts`). Values are
`on` or `off`, case-insensitive with surrounding whitespace ignored. Unset or
blank strings mean OFF. Unsupported strings and defined non-string values
throw an error naming the setting; the host owns startup/request error handling.
All five live in one module (`src/card/parity/strict-parity-flags.ts`), read
through `strictParityFlagsFromEnv` / `configureStrictParity`, the same
arrangement the sign-in policy uses.
An explicitly configured flag object takes precedence over process environment
until `configureStrictParity(undefined)` clears it; changing environment
variables alone does not replace that object.

| Setting (`DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_` prefix) | OFF (default) | ON (selected reference path) | Recorded evidence |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `SIGNIN_DETAILED_ERRORS` | Uniform failure wording and the unknown-address sent-reset redirect under the default sign-in policy; this wording is not a proof against timing leaks | Field-specific recorded sign-in/reset errors: "Unrecognized email.", "Wrong password", 422 "EMAIL: not recognized" | battery `signin-unknown`, `signin-wrong-password`, `reset-unknown-email` |
| `REVISION_RESTORE_500` | The measured File/Image revision restore proceeds (303) | Selects the recorded crash path: 500 with the deck's `server_error` main slot and no stored change | battery `restore-file-revision` and location-history control `admin-deny-signup` |
| `SKIN_CREATE_500` | The measured non-skin parent refuses with 422 "ABORT: not a valid theme ..." | Selects the recorded crash path: 500 with the deck's `server_error` main slot and no stored change | battery `admin-create-skin-bad-parent` |
| `SCRIPT_BUNDLE_CLOCK_NAMES` | The compiled bundle's bytes determine its filename | Ruby's stand-in action id `Time.now.to_i` names the file by wall-clock second; repeated saves within that second can share a name | battery `get-home` and identical-byte re-stores `admin-update-current-skin-colors`, `admin-delete-script-card` |
| `MAIL_UNFOLLOW_REFUSAL` | Notification mail uses the bare reference link - the mail's bytes are the reference's in EVERY configuration (issue 111); the measured fixture's dashboard/follow-rule writes use the default path | The measured dashboard write refuses, and follow-rule creates check the trunk parts they materialize; the mail's bytes are the same as OFF | `tests/card/actions/follow-rule-create-side-fold-switch.test.ts`; separate `unfollow-from-mail` and board journeys; synthetic-v1 mail comparisons (`tests/card/notify/notification-unfollow-link-parity.test.ts`); absent from `tools/parity-side-effects/scenarios.json` |

To select all five recorded behaviours, set each of these host settings to
`on`:

```text
DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_SIGNIN_DETAILED_ERRORS=on
DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_REVISION_RESTORE_500=on
DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_SKIN_CREATE_500=on
DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_SCRIPT_BUNDLE_CLOCK_NAMES=on
DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_MAIL_UNFOLLOW_REFUSAL=on
```

**Evidence limitations:** the [historical v29 roll receipt](runs/full-candidate-v29-lanes-roll-2026-10-27.md)
at `83e50ad639dcf4e04efa90323f13fc7d9d5ac1af` is retained as history, not
current release evidence. Its side-effects battery has no follow/unfollow
notification case and cannot verify the fifth flag. Its strict `get-home`
expectation includes a TS file-list pin for fixture-clock differences, which
must not be described as raw Ruby file equality.

That receipt's browser journey comparator masked calendar dates, durations
and relative times. Its earlier pass figure is invalidated as strict-text
evidence. The repaired
`reference-harness/browser/lib/observe.mjs` preserves those values in title,
body and main text; only whitespace and the local test origin are normalized
there. Clock differences now remain differences. The structure and URL rules
remain separate and unchanged.

The corrected normalizer cannot recover text already replaced by `<time>`
in saved observations. A fresh grade requires retained original rendered
text for both sides or a new isolated capture. Running the comparator again
on previously masked JSON does not verify this correction. No replacement
journey score is claimed here. The focused tests below demonstrate that
both literal-date and literal-duration counterexamples now fail strict text
comparison with valid synthetic evidence; they do not rerun a real deck.

**Crash-response scope:** the selected strict path must render the crash
inside the deck's page layout, not return a bare error fragment. The recorded
main-slot bytes, whole-page bytes, headers and persistence are distinct
checks. Recorded head assets, root URLs and integrity attributes differ
between configurations, so a matching main slot does not prove whole-page
equality. The default-mode `strictErases` exemption must not hide strict-mode
body failures. Keep the original bare-fragment counterexample and the
comparator's actual declarations with each run.

## Independent host settings

These settings select separate axes; enabling the five behavior flags does
not supply any of them.

| Setting | Current Node meaning | What it does not prove |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `DECKO_DECK_PROFILE=stock` | In `vm-staging-server.mjs`, only the exact string `stock` selects the stock host path; unset and every other value select the legacy host path. This is not a validated two-value enum | DSL/mod completeness or compatibility with every older deck |
| `DECKO_SERIALIZER_ERA=stock` or `live` | The Node host trims its environment string; unset/blank leaves the current configuration. Exact `stock` clears this axis, exact `live` selects it; other values throw. Separate from deck profile and shell | Reference-version provenance or rendering equality |
| `DECKO_PUBLIC_PAGE_SHELL_FILE` | Deployment-owned public shell configuration | Engine semantic compatibility or source-backed permission to publish its content |
| `DECKO_LEGACY_ASSETS_FILE` | Validated media origin, main asset paths and same-card revision map | Media existence or availability; arbitrary authored URL/text equivalence |

The finite asset-parser/script-role counterexamples were independently
accepted in #67 at `d4eefc8de35fc18376c584ba694198d190f1c812`; that acceptance
does not establish universal HTML conformance, full-host configuration or media
availability. Explicit Node deck-origin wiring remains separate
work (#68); `DECKO_ORIGIN` is an existing account/mail setting, not a documented
substitute for that rendering configuration. A deployment must pin its exact
source, build, data, settings and reference before it claims a comparison.

The parser also rejects defined non-string settings instead of silently
treating them as OFF. The original failing control is retained in
`.runtime/adversarial-repair/strict-switch-nonstring-before.log`; the corrected
settings, sign-in, write-route, asset-naming and follow-rule controls are in
`strict-switch-value-admission-controls.log`. These are local finite controls;
independent adversarial review and current target acceptance remain separate.

## Relationship to the sign-in policy switches

`DECKO_SIGNIN_UNIFORM_FAILURE` (docs/SECURITY.md) keeps its own meaning beside
the strict surface: `off` already restores the per-field messages. The strict
switch wins while it is ON - it forces the uniform-failure policy off for the
sign-in ladder and the forgot-password trigger alike, whatever
`DECKO_SIGNIN_UNIFORM_FAILURE` says - so the recorded Ruby answers are
reachable through one consistent name without disturbing the existing
documented switch.

The five flags do not configure password hashing, origin checks, sign-in
throttling, session revocation or first-run setup tokens. Those independent
policies retain the meanings and compatibility costs in [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md).
For example, a shared-engine migration needs its separately selected password
policy: Ruby cannot verify a password written or upgraded to the port's default
PBKDF2 format. Enabling the strict flags does not change that fact.

The canonical TS reference adapter is a different configuration from a default
host. `reference-harness/adapters/ts/adapter.ts` selects `legacy-sha1` by default
through `DECKO_HARNESS_PASSWORD_HASH` (only exact `legacy-sha1` or `pbkdf2` is
accepted) and unconditionally sets uniform sign-in failures, throttling and
session revocation OFF. Its results must carry that configuration; they do not
verify the default host's security policies. The adapter also selects
`DECKO_STRICT_PARITY_MAIL_UNFOLLOW_REFUSAL=on` when the variable is unset, so its
notification mail carries Ruby's bare unfollow link byte for byte; an explicit
`off` probes the default behaviour and reports seven synthetic-v1 side-effect
differences. The adapter records the effective value as `mail_unfollow_refusal`
in its ready message. The five strict flags remain independent of these adapter
overrides; hosts and the engine still default every flag off.

